{"entities":[{"text":"All-trans retinoic acid-induced","label":"Chemical","id":""},{"text":"All-trans retinoic acid","label":"Chemical","id":""},{"text":"ATRA","label":"Chemical","id":""},{"text":"ATRA-induced","label":"Chemical","id":""},{"text":"inflammatory myositis","label":"Disease","id":""},{"text":"acute promyelocytic leukemia","label":"Disease","id":""},{"text":"acute promyelocytic leukemia","label":"Disease","id":""},{"text":"APL","label":"Disease","id":""},{"text":"myositis","label":"Disease","id":""},{"text":"APL","label":"Disease","id":""}],"entity_aliases":[{"text":"ATRA","id":"1","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"All-trans retinoic acid","id":"1","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"myositis","id":"1","label":"Disease"},{"text":"inflammatory myositis","id":"1","label":"Disease"},{"text":"APL","id":"2","label":"Disease"},{"text":"acute promyelocytic leukemia","id":"2","label":"Disease"}],"filtered_entities":[{"text":"All-trans retinoic acid","id":"1","label":"Chemical"},{"text":"inflammatory myositis","id":"1","label":"Disease"},{"text":"acute promyelocytic leukemia","id":"2","label":"Disease"}],"gold_relations":"ATRA | CID | myositis","labels":"Chemical,Disease","metrics":{"precision":1.0,"recall":1.0,"f1":1.0,"tp":1,"fp":0,"fn":0,"rel_precision":1.0,"rel_recall":1.0,"rel_f1":1.0,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":0,"rel_fn":0},"pairs":[{"head":"All-trans retinoic acid","tail":"inflammatory myositis","head_id":"1","tail_id":"1"},{"head":"All-trans retinoic acid","tail":"acute promyelocytic leukemia","head_id":"1","tail_id":"2"}],"relations":["1 | 1"],"text":"All-trans retinoic acid-induced inflammatory myositis in a patient with acute promyelocytic leukemia. All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA), a component of standard therapy for acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), is associated with potentially serious but treatable adverse effects involving numerous organ systems, including rare skeletal muscle involvement. Only a handful of cases of ATRA-induced myositis in children have been reported, and none in the radiology literature. We present such a case in a 15-year-old boy with APL, where recognition of imaging findings played a crucial role in making the diagnosis and facilitated prompt, effective treatment."}